### How does HashSet work internally?

HashSet delegates everything to a HashMap. Understanding this is the key to understanding all Set behavior.

Here is the actual structure from OpenJDK, simplified:

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class HashSetInternalsDemo {
    // Simplified from OpenJDK source
    static class SimpleHashSet<E> {
        private final HashMap<E, Object> map;
        private static final Object PRESENT = new Object();

        public SimpleHashSet() {
            this.map = new HashMap<>();
        }

        public SimpleHashSet(int initialCapacity) {
            this.map = new HashMap<>(initialCapacity);
        }

        public boolean add(E e) {
            return map.put(e, PRESENT) == null;
            // put returns null → key was NEW → return true
            // put returns PRESENT → key EXISTED → return false
        }

        public boolean contains(Object o) {
            return map.containsKey(o);
        }

        public boolean remove(Object o) {
            return map.remove(o) == PRESENT;
        }

        public int size() {
            return map.size();
        }

        public Iterator<E> iterator() {
            return map.keySet().iterator();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<String> accounts = new HashSet<>();
        System.out.println("add SAV2001: " + accounts.add("SAV2001")); // true
        System.out.println("add CUR3001: " + accounts.add("CUR3001")); // true
        System.out.println("add SAV2001: " + accounts.add("SAV2001")); // false – duplicate!
        System.out.println("contains SAV2001: " + accounts.contains("SAV2001")); // true
        System.out.println("size: " + accounts.size()); // 2
        System.out.println("remove SAV2001: " + accounts.remove("SAV2001")); // true
        System.out.println("size after remove: " + accounts.size()); // 1
    }
}
```

```text
add SAV2001: true
add CUR3001: true
add SAV2001: false
contains SAV2001: true
size: 2
remove SAV2001: true
size after remove: 1
```

### Why does the dummy PRESENT value exist?

HashMap requires both a key and a value. Since HashSet only cares about keys (the elements), every entry shares the same value: a single `Object` instance called PRESENT. This wastes no additional memory per entry because PRESENT is a static final field – one object for the entire application.

The PRESENT object is never returned to user code. It exists solely to satisfy HashMap's key-value contract.

### What HashMap properties does HashSet inherit?

Because HashSet delegates to HashMap, it inherits all of HashMap's characteristics:

| Property | Value | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Default capacity | 16 | HashMap default |
| Load factor | 0.75 | HashMap default |
| Resize trigger | size > capacity * 0.75 | HashMap |
| Treeification | Chain >= 8 nodes | HashMap (Java 8+) |
| Null element | One null allowed | HashMap allows one null key |
| Hash function | hashCode() ^ (hashCode >>> 16) | HashMap spreading |
| Iteration order | Unpredictable | HashMap |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class HashSetPropertiesDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // One null element allowed (HashMap allows one null key)
        Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();
        set.add(null);
        set.add(null); // duplicate null – ignored
        System.out.println("Null in set: " + set.contains(null)); // true
        System.out.println("Size: " + set.size()); // 1

        // Pre-sizing works the same as HashMap
        int expected = 10_000;
        Set<String> preSized = new HashSet<>(expected * 4 / 3 + 1);
        // Avoids resizing for up to 10K elements
    }
}
```

```text
Null in set: true
Size: 1
```

### Why must elements implement hashCode() and equals()?

Since elements are used as HashMap keys, they must implement a proper `hashCode()` and `equals()` contract. Without these, two logically equal objects are treated as different set members.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class HashCodeEqualsDemo {
    // WRONG: no hashCode/equals override
    static class BadAccount {
        String id;
        BadAccount(String id) { this.id = id; }
    }

    // CORRECT: proper hashCode and equals
    static class GoodAccount {
        final String id;
        GoodAccount(String id) { this.id = id; }
        @Override public int hashCode() { return id.hashCode(); }
        @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
            return o instanceof GoodAccount ga && ga.id.equals(this.id);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Without hashCode/equals: duplicates sneak in
        Set<BadAccount> bad = new HashSet<>();
        bad.add(new BadAccount("SAV2001"));
        bad.add(new BadAccount("SAV2001"));
        System.out.println("Bad set size: " + bad.size()); // 2 – wrong!

        // With hashCode/equals: uniqueness works
        Set<GoodAccount> good = new HashSet<>();
        good.add(new GoodAccount("SAV2001"));
        good.add(new GoodAccount("SAV2001"));
        System.out.println("Good set size: " + good.size()); // 1 – correct!
    }
}
```

```text
Bad set size: 2
Good set size: 1
```

### How does the same pattern apply to LinkedHashSet and TreeSet?

| Set | Internal Map | Ordering | Performance |
|---|---|---|---|
| HashSet | HashMap | No ordering | O(1) |
| LinkedHashSet | LinkedHashMap | Insertion order | O(1) |
| TreeSet | TreeMap | Sorted order | O(log n) |

LinkedHashSet extends HashSet but passes a LinkedHashMap to the superclass constructor. TreeSet wraps a TreeMap. The pattern is identical: elements are map keys, values are PRESENT (or the map's internal mechanics).

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class SetOrderingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = List.of("CUR3001", "SAV2001", "FD4001");

        System.out.println("HashSet:       " + new HashSet<>(accounts));
        System.out.println("LinkedHashSet: " + new LinkedHashSet<>(accounts));
        System.out.println("TreeSet:       " + new TreeSet<>(accounts));
    }
}
```

```text
HashSet:       [FD4001, CUR3001, SAV2001]
LinkedHashSet: [CUR3001, SAV2001, FD4001]
TreeSet:       [CUR3001, FD4001, SAV2001]
```

### What about memory overhead?

Each element in a HashSet is stored as a HashMap Node: object header (12-16 bytes) + hash (4 bytes) + key reference (4 bytes) + value reference to PRESENT (4 bytes) + next pointer (4 bytes) + padding. Total: approximately 32-48 bytes per element.

The PRESENT object itself is a single static instance shared by all entries. It adds no per-element overhead.

For memory-sensitive applications with large sets, consider alternatives like a `BitSet` (for integers), `EnumSet` (for enum constants, uses bit vector internally), or a sorted array with binary search.

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: Not overriding hashCode when overriding equals

```java
// WRONG: equals overridden but hashCode not
class Account {
    String id;
    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
        return o instanceof Account a && a.id.equals(this.id);
    }
    // No hashCode override – uses Object.hashCode (identity)
    // Two equal accounts may have different hash codes → different buckets
    // → HashSet treats them as different elements!
}
```

#### Mistake 2: Using ArrayList.contains instead of HashSet.contains

```java
// WRONG: O(n) per check for 50K elements
List<String> blockedAccounts = new ArrayList<>(/* 50K entries */);
if (blockedAccounts.contains(accountId)) { } // scans entire list!

// CORRECT: O(1) per check
Set<String> blockedAccounts = new HashSet<>(/* 50K entries */);
if (blockedAccounts.contains(accountId)) { } // hash lookup
```

### What about thread safety?

HashSet is not thread-safe. For concurrent access:

- `Collections.synchronizedSet(new HashSet<>())` – single lock wrapper.
- `ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()` – lock-free concurrent set backed by ConcurrentHashMap.
- `CopyOnWriteArraySet` – for read-heavy, write-rare scenarios.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

// Thread-safe set with ConcurrentHashMap
Set<String> concurrentSet = ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();
concurrentSet.add("SAV2001"); // thread-safe
concurrentSet.contains("SAV2001"); // thread-safe, lock-free
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **EnumSet optimization.** When your set contains enum constants, always use `EnumSet` instead of HashSet. EnumSet uses a bit vector internally (one bit per enum constant) and is far faster and more memory-efficient than any hash-based set.

- **Set.of() produces an immutable set.** `Set.of("A", "B", "C")` returns an unmodifiable set that does not allow null, duplicates, or modification. The implementation is not a HashSet – it uses a specialized compact representation.

- **Collectors.toUnmodifiableSet().** In streams, `collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableSet())` creates an immutable set. It rejects null and duplicates.

- **HashSet serialization.** HashSet has custom serialization that writes only the elements (not the HashMap internal structure). On deserialization, it reconstructs the HashMap from the elements.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the internal data structure of HashSet?" – A HashMap where elements are keys and values are a dummy PRESENT constant.
- "Can HashSet contain null?" – Yes, one null element (because HashMap allows one null key).
- "What happens if you add a duplicate to HashSet?" – add() returns false and the set is unchanged.
- "Why must elements implement hashCode and equals?" – Because they are used as HashMap keys.
